# Findings the full-history secret scan raises that are NOT secrets.
#
# Each line is a gitleaks FINGERPRINT - commit:file:rule:line - so it suppresses
# exactly one finding in exactly one historical commit. No rule is disabled and
# no forward blind spot is created: the same rule firing on a new commit, or on
# a different line, is a new fingerprint and still fails.
#
# WHY THERE ARE ENTRIES HERE AT ALL. The CI secret scan was decorative until
# 2026-09-14 (it ran gitleaks with --staged on a checkout that stages nothing,
# so every run reported "0 commits scanned"). Its first real pass over main
# raised ten findings, all in unit-test fixtures, all absent from the current
# tree. Decoded before being listed here - a token was never pasted into this
# file, only read from the historical blob and its claims inspected:
#
# * frontend/tests/unit/stores/auth.spec.ts, rule jwt, four commits between
#   2025-12-05 and 2025-12-26 (lines 139/141/166 then 538/540). Two distinct
#   tokens. One decodes to header {alg HS256} payload {sub "1234567890"} - the
#   jwt.io demo token, signed with its published demo secret. The other decodes
#   to payload {sub "refresh"} with an 18-character signature, which no real
#   HS256 token can have (43). Both were hand-built to exercise the auth store's
#   decode/expiry paths; neither ever authenticated to anything.
# * frontend/tests/unit/schemas/api.schemas.spec.ts:299, rule generic-api-key,
#   2025-12-03: a 36-character JWT-shaped string in a schema-validation fixture
#   (a real JWT is over 100). Fixture.
#
# [!] TWO FINDINGS FROM THE SAME SCAN ARE DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS FILE, AND
# MUST NOT BE ADDED. DEPLOYMENT_STATUS.md, commits of 2025-11-06 and
# 2025-11-26 on main, carried a production database password under a heading
# that said so. That is a real credential in public history. The code-two
# policy for a real credential is ROTATE FIRST - a fingerprint here would make
# the gate green while the password stayed live, which is the exact failure
# this scan exists to prevent. Those two fingerprints may be added only after
# the rotation is done and recorded, in the form the estate used for the Brave
# keys: "revoked <date>", never "not a secret". Until then this repo's gate is
# red for exactly one reason, and that reason is correct.
#
# [!] Before adding any line here, decide which kind it is. This file says
# "this was never a secret" or "this secret is dead"; it never says "this
# secret is handled".

